{"ex-E402-1":{"number":"1","title":"Sound intensity","question":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a point source of sound. Position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is at 5 m from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The sound intensity measured at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 4.5 × 10<sup>−6</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup>. Find the power of the source.</p>","answer":"<p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4π<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4π × 5<sup>2</sup> × (4.5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) = 1.41 × 10<sup>−3</sup> W</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>How will the intensity at X change if the power of the source is doubled?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>It doubles. At the same distance, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>∝</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, so the new intensity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>new</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":3},"id":"ex-E402-1","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-2":{"number":"2","title":"Sound intensity (using proportion)","question":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a point source of sound. Positions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and 3<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> respectively. The sound intensity measured at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 4.5 × 10<sup>−6</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup>. Find the sound intensity measured at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ∝ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (4.5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) = 5 × 10<sup>−7</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":13},"id":"ex-E402-2","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-3":{"number":"3","title":"Sound intensity and sound intensity level","question":"<p>Edward hears a sound from outside the window.</p><p>Given: threshold of hearing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 10<sup>−12</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup></p><p>(a) Suppose the sound reaching Edward has an intensity of 10<sup>−7</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup>. What is the sound intensity level as heard by Edward?</p><p>(b) Edward walks towards the window and stops next to it. If he hears a sound of 60 dB, what is the intensity of sound received by</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a2e17999dd6f845ee7b45f2f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:184.9px\"/></p><p>him?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10 log (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = 10 log (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = 50 dB</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10 log (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>)</p><p>60 = 10 log (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>)</p><p>6 = log (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>)</p><p>10<sup>6</sup> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10<sup>−6</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p><b>Mathematics</b></p><p>Logarithm p.179</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 10","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":20},"id":"ex-E402-3","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-4":{"number":"4","title":"Curves of equal loudness","question":"<p>The following figure shows some curves of equal loudness of John.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/44443c835d0dd1efc976c207.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:521.9px\"/></p><p>(a) What is the phon number of a 200-Hz note at 40 dB?</p><p>(b) Compare the loudness of a 100-Hz note at 50 dB and a 10 000-Hz note at 40 dB as perceived by John.</p><p>(c) Which range of frequencies of sound is John’s ear more sensitive to, 100−200 Hz or 1000−2000 Hz?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 20</p><p>(b) The 10 000-Hz note at 40 dB is louder.</p><p>(c) 1000−2000 Hz</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>This is because the sound of 200 Hz and 40 dB lies on the curve which passes through the sound of 1000 Hz and 20 dB.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>This is because the sound of 100 Hz and 50 dB is between 10 phons and 20 phons, while the sound of 10 000 Hz and 40 dB is between 20 phons and 30 phons.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>This is because the part of an equal loudness curve within this frequency range is lower, meaning that sound within this frequency range needs a lower sound intensity level to provide the same perceived loudness.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":39},"id":"ex-E402-4","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-5":{"number":"5","title":"Hearing loss","question":"<p>The following figure shows the threshold of hearing of Mandy and a normal person.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/de6731435aee6d70390cfb43.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:413.9px\"/></p><p>(a) Is Mandy’s hearing loss more severe at low pitch sound or high pitch sound?</p><p>(b) Explain your answer in (a).</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Low pitch sound</p><p>(b) The curve of Mandy is shifted upwards by a large amount from the curve of a normal person in the low frequency part, which means that the sound intensity level she needs to hear at low frequencies is much higher than a normal person.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 15","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":57},"id":"ex-E402-5","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-7":{"number":"7","title":"Proportion of energy reflected and transmitted","question":"<p>An ultrasound pulse travels towards the spleen as shown. The acoustic impedances of fat and spleen are 1.38 × 10<sup>6</sup> kg m<sup>−2</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> and</p><p>1.65 × 10<sup>6</sup> kg m<sup>−2</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> respectively.</p><p>(a) Find the intensity reflection coefficient at interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find the proportion of energy of the pulse</p><p>(i) reflected, (ii) transmitted if the pulse is incident on interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> normally.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a909239f722efb34d0f44b25.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:168.7px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) α = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.007 94</p><p>(b) (i) Proportion of energy reflected = α = 0.007 94</p><p>(ii) Proportion of energy transmitted = 1 − α = 1 − 0.007 94 = 0.992 06</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>1 What is the intensity reflection coefficient at interface Q?</p><p>2 If the intensity of ultrasound pulse incident on interface P is 100 W m<sup>-2</sup>, what are the intensities of the reflected pulse and the transmitted pulse?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>1 Q is a spleen–fat interface. Interchanging the impedances does not change <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the same as at P.</p><p>2 At normal incidence, neglecting absorption at the interface, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 25","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":66},"id":"ex-E402-7","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-8":{"number":"8","title":"Depth and thickness","question":"<p>An ultrasound pulse is emitted by the transducer at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 as shown. The</p><p>echoes from interfaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are detected by the transducer at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 15.4 μs</p><p>and 21.2 μs respectively. Assume that the speed of ultrasound along its path is 1550 m s<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Find the depth (in mm) of interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from the transducer.</p><p>(b) Find the thickness (in mm) of the structure between interfaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c5172e4eaf5aef30f8a230f9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:143.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1550 × (15.4 × 10<sup>–6</sup>) = 0.0119 m = 11.9 mm</p><p>(b) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1550 × (21.2 × 10<sup>–6</sup> – 15.4 × 10<sup>–6</sup>) = 0.004 50 m = 4.50 mm</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 30","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":85},"id":"ex-E402-8","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-9":{"number":"9","title":"Finding thickness of a structure","question":"<p>A patient is having an ultrasound scan for his eye (Fig a). Figure b shows the A-scan image obtained.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/afeb2c01ba41bfc1eaae53f4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:510.8px\"/></p><p>Assume that the speed of ultrasound in the eye is 1550 m s<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> corresponds to the retina. Find the distance (in mm) of the retina from the transducer.</p><p>(b) (i) Which spike corresponds to the front surface of the lens? Which spike corresponds to the back surface?</p><p>(ii) Find the thickness of the lens (in mm).</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1550 × (25 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) = 0.0194 m = 19.4 mm</p><p>(b) (i) Spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> corresponds to the front surface.</p><p>Spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> corresponds to the back surface.</p><p>(ii) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1550 × (8 × 10<sup>−6</sup> − 4 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) = 0.0031 m = 3.1 mm</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>How will the answer in (a) and (b)(ii) change if the speed of ultrasound in the lens is 1500 m s<sup>-1</sup> (still 1550 m s<sup>-1</sup> in other parts)?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Use the same recorded echo times: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The round-trip time within the lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b)(ii) Lens thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, instead of 3.10 mm.</p><p>(a) The remaining round-trip travel time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Thus the retina distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. It is 0.100 mm less than the unrounded original value 19.375 mm.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 32","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":96},"id":"ex-E402-9","chapter":"E402"},"ex-E402-10":{"number":"10","title":"Intensity of echoes in A-scan image","question":"<p>A narrow beam of ultrasound pulse is emitted from the transducer to perform an A-scan (Fig a). Spikes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> correspond to the detection of echoes from interfaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> respectively.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5d04a8449ced0b5ba9fed497.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:488.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a large amplitude. What can you tell about the intensity reflection coefficient of interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>? Explain.</p><p>(b) Spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a much smaller amplitude. Why?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The large amplitude of spike <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> indicates a high intensity of the echo from interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, and thus a large intensity reflection coefficient of this interface.</p><p>(b) Interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> reflects nearly all of the ultrasound.</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>1 What can you tell about the acoustic impedance of structure S?</p><p>2 Does the small amplitude of spike N indicate a small intensity reflection coefficient of interface Q?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>1 The acoustic impedance of S differs greatly from that of fat. A reflection coefficient close to 1 requires a large impedance mismatch. It could be much greater or much smaller than the impedance of fat; the echo intensity alone does not distinguish these possibilities.</p><p>2 No. P and Q separate the same two materials in reverse order, so their normal-incidence reflection coefficients are equal. Most of the incident energy is already reflected at P, leaving little to reach Q. The returning Q echo must also pass through P, reducing it further. Neglecting attenuation and multiple echoes, its received intensity is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can be small even when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is large.</p>","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E402  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"E402 Examples.docx","sha256":"8f8a50c7586476bd224f73b5ebaf8a12fbf8ec4a26deb9d8fc736913f391bd10","block":115},"id":"ex-E402-10","chapter":"E402"}}